CORE IDENTITY
01  ART-DIRECTION NORTH STAR

The project should look like an optimistic corporate future designed around 1999–2003 rather than a contemporary 
sci-fi world dressed in retro references. The visual language is clean, cool, sharply designed and intentionally 
artificial. The world is aspirational on the surface: white towers, blue skies, immaculate signage, glass, brushed 
metal and luminous office light. The game itself then abuses that immaculate world with slapstick PvP chaos.
The key contrast is deliberate: the environment is airy and architecturally clean, while characters are graphic, 
exaggerated and immediately readable. The result should feel like an early-2000s design agency built the city, a 
fashion illustrator designed the employees, and then somebody started a workplace war.
THE SHORTEST POSSIBLE BRIEF
2001 corporate futurism outside. Sharp graphic lawyers inside. Clean enough to feel expensive; strange enough to feel 
fictional; simple enough to read during chaos.

Four visual pillars
STATUS
ITEM
HANDOVER NOTE
LOCKED
Corporate futurism
White architecture, cobalt sky, glass, clean geometric branding, controlled blue/cyan accents, sparse futuristic 
infrastructure.
LOCKED
Graphic characters
Long-limbed, angular, fashion-led characters with simplified faces and deliberate cel-shadow shapes.
LOCKED
Soft-club atmosphere
Air, negative space, thin graphic lines, understated technology, cool light, fashion/editorial influence rather than 
loud cyberpunk.
LOCKED
Straight-faced corporate comedy
Corporate/legal naming and presentation stay serious. The joke comes from what players do inside the serious visual 
system.

What this is not
PROTECT THE IDENTITY
■ Cold, clean and highly controlled.
■ Large simple shapes before surface detail.
■ Blue/white/charcoal as structural colours.
■ Fashion and editorial design influence.
■ Hard graphic character shadows inside softer environments.
AVOID DRIFTING INTO
■ Vaporwave nostalgia.
■ Frutiger Aero greenery/bubbles.
■ Generic neon cyberpunk.
■ Overly cute anime proportions.
■ Modern glossy anime-game cel shading.
■ Dense sci-fi greeble/detail for its own sake.

REFERENCE HIERARCHY
02  PRIMARY REFERENCE PLATES

These four user-selected references define the core graphic and environmental lane. They are not to be copied 
literally. They establish the project’s rules for colour, air, geometry, typography and visual restraint.


Reference 01 — early-2000s editorial game graphic: thin line systems, sparse copy, blue field, flare and airy negative 
space.
Reference 02 — Gen X Soft Club / techno-minimal composition: pale surfaces, thin geometry, sky photography, soft 
translucent forms.

Reference 03 — white field, sky inserts, black technical linework and abstract sculptural form. Strong example of 
graphic restraint.
PRIMARY WORLD REFERENCE
Reference 04 — the environmental anchor

Reference 04 — PRIMARY environmental anchor: white-blue corporate city, immaculate massing, giant graphic branding, 
clear roads, cobalt sky and restrained cyan accents.
This image is the strongest single environment reference for the project. The most important lesson is not “make a 
futuristic city”; it is how little information each building needs. White slabs, dark window bands, one large blue 
graphic, tiny signage and clean spacing do most of the work.
■ Take: large architectural masses, blue-white hierarchy, empty sky, polished commercial mood, billboard-scale fashion 
imagery, graphic branding integrated into buildings.
■ Do not take: photoreal detail density, generic sci-fi clutter, or the idea that every surface needs a futuristic 
prop attached to it.
■ Gameplay translation: across the tower gap, players should read as dark graphic figures against bright windows and 
sky rather than disappearing into noisy backgrounds.
Secondary references by function
STATUS
ITEM
HANDOVER NOTE
LOCKED
Jet Set Radio / JSRF
Use for silhouette, long-limbed proportions, oversized graphic hands/shoes and instant readability. Do not inherit its 
graffiti palette wholesale.
LOCKED
Persona
Use as a lesson in cohesive graphic identity: UI, typography, character presentation and world branding must feel 
designed together.
LOCKED
Gen X Soft Club
Use for coolness, air, restraint, transparency, thin lines, blue-white space and early-digital editorial tone.
WORKING
Killer7-like shadow logic
Use the principle of ruthless simplification: a base colour plus a major designed shadow shape instead of many soft 
lighting bands.
WORKING
Rez / Wipeout-era futurism
Use selectively for abstract corporate-tech atmosphere, signage discipline and synthetic optimism — never as a direct 
style copy.

COLOUR
03  COLOUR SYSTEM

The world should feel brand-managed. Colour is not decorative noise: it is a controlled system that makes the 
environment feel corporate, legible and intentionally designed. The palette should skew cool. Warmth is allowed when 
motivated by a specific prop, skin tone or gameplay accent, but it should not turn the project pastel or cozy.
SWATCH
NAME
HEX
USE

Paper White
#F3F4F4
Primary architectural field; walls, signage, UI space.

Silver Grey
#C9CED3
Secondary panels, office surfaces, quiet separators.

Deep Navy
#111B38
Dark structural anchor; suits, window bands, text.

Corporate Cobalt
#244FB9
Brand colour; large graphic areas and major accents.

Electric Blue
#2879EF
Brighter active accent; UI highlights and selected signage.

Cyan
#20D4E6
Small luminous emphasis; indicators, lines, rare highlights.

Near Black
#101116
Maximum contrast; text, shoes, selected equipment.
Colour behaviour
■ White and pale grey should occupy the most visual area in architecture and interfaces.
■ Cobalt is structural: use it in large confident blocks rather than sprinkling small blue objects everywhere.
■ Cyan is an accent, not a base colour. It works best as a thin line, indicator, light strip or tiny digital emphasis.
■ Black/navy provide silhouette contrast and stop the palette becoming soft or pastel.
■ Character-specific or gameplay accents can sit on top of this system, but should be intentionally rationed.
COLOUR DANGER
Do not let “Y2K” become pink chrome, rainbow gradients, candy translucency or vaporwave. The desired Y2K is corporate, 
architectural and cold.

LIGHTING
04  LIGHTING &amp; ATMOSPHERE

Lighting is where the “airy” half of the style lives. The world can be soft, luminous and slightly hazy even while 
character shading remains hard-edged. This contrast is essential.
Exterior daylight
■ Prefer strong blue sky, pale atmospheric perspective and bright architectural whites.
■ Keep the skyline readable as large blocks; distant haze should simplify rather than add noise.
■ Glass can tint toward blue/cyan but must not become a mirror that hides cross-building gameplay.
■ Bloom is restrained. Bright edges may glow slightly, but the frame should remain crisp and graphic.
Interior office light
■ Cool fluorescent / LED-like illumination with broad soft ambient fill.
■ White surfaces should remain white or cool grey, not warm cream.
■ Harder directional accents can carve characters and props out of the soft ambient field.
■ Service rooms may be darker and more enclosed, but should still belong to the same cool corporate world.
■ Avoid dramatic cyberpunk coloured light unless a specific event or gameplay effect requires it.
LIGHTING FORMULA
SOFT WORLD LIGHT + HARD CHARACTER VALUE BREAKS. The environment supplies air; the character shader supplies graphic 
punch.

RENDERING
05  CEL-SHADING LANGUAGE

The character shader must not read as a generic modern anime toon shader. The project needs fewer bands, fewer effects 
and stronger decisions. The target is editorial illustration logic translated into 3D.
Core shader rule
BASE → CUT SHADOW
A material should usually read as one base field plus one major hard-edged shadow field. Additional shading is 
exceptional, not default.

■ One primary shadow threshold. A second threshold only when a material or face genuinely needs it.
■ No smooth character gradients as the default look.
■ Shadows may shift slightly colder rather than simply becoming black.
■ Highlights should appear as designed shapes: a hard streak on leather, a pale plane on hair, a sharp lapel 
reflection.
■ Outlines are selective. Use them to protect silhouette or clarify overlaps, not as a thick black border around every 
object.
■ Allow art-directed cheats. If a prettier shadow better explains the pose, silhouette or face, visual clarity wins 
over physical correctness.
Material-specific treatment
STATUS
ITEM
HANDOVER NOTE
LOCKED
Suit fabric
Mostly flat. Broad value separation, minimal specular noise, almost no micro-normal texture.
LOCKED
Skin
Clean base tone plus one controlled cool shadow mass. Keep facial planes readable without painting realistic pores.
LOCKED
Leather / polished shoes
Hard graphic highlight shapes are allowed; still avoid smooth glossy CG falloff.
LOCKED
Hair
Large mass first, one or two highlight planes at most. Hair detail must not dissolve the silhouette.
WORKING
Glass / metal props
Environment props can be more conventionally PBR than characters, but their material response should remain simple and 
controlled.

Shader anti-goals
TARGET
■ Graphic flat planes.
■ One bold shadow shape.
■ Material-specific hard highlights.
■ Selective contour assistance.
■ Readable from gameplay distance.
AVOID
■ Five-band anime ramps.
■ Permanent thick ink outlines.
■ Rim light on every material.
■ Face-map complexity that makes every character glossy.
■ Painterly gradients fighting the geometry.

CHARACTERS
06  CHARACTER DESIGN SYSTEM

Characters should feel like fashion-forward corporate caricatures, not mascots. The agreed proportion language leans 
toward Jet Set Radio: compact torso, long legs and limbs, graphic hands and shoes, and a silhouette that can be 
recognised through a window from the opposite tower.
Proportion rules
■ Long legs and relatively compact torso.
■ Lean, nimble builds rather than chibi or bulky heroic anatomy.
■ Hands may be oversized enough to read animation and weapon poses clearly.
■ Shoes can be chunky or elongated as a graphic counterweight, but should still feel sleek rather than toy-like.
■ Heads and facial features stay stylised, but the whole character must not feel “cute.”
■ The male blazer should sit in a controlled fitted zone: not cropped to the waist, not oversized or slouchy.
■ The female silhouette can be pushed slightly for character, but not exaggerated into caricature that breaks the 
shared base style.
Shape language
■ Angular over rounded: pointed collars, triangular lapels, sharp shoulder cuts, clean crease lines and tapering 
shapes.
■ Use a few large clothing shapes rather than many decorative seams, buckles or panels.
■ Negative space between limbs matters. Avoid poses and silhouettes that collapse into one dark mass.
■ Each character should have one or two memorable silhouette decisions, not ten surface details.
READABILITY TEST
If a character becomes a 100-pixel-tall silhouette against a bright window, you should still understand their stance, 
major clothing shape and which way they are facing.

CORPORATE FASHION
07  CLOTHING &amp; WARDROBE LANGUAGE

The default wardrobe is corporate tailoring treated as graphic fashion design. It should look recognisably 
professional while being sharp enough to support the project’s stylisation.
■ Neutral suit templates form the baseline for player characters.
■ Jackets are clean and fitted with deliberate lapel geometry. Avoid both extreme crop and oversized contemporary 
streetwear proportions.
■ Trousers may flare or widen toward the shoe to reinforce the long-leg silhouette and late-90s/early-2000s influence.
■ Shirts, ties and belts should use strong simple shapes; tiny tailoring detail should not dominate.
■ Accessories should feel like corporate/fashion objects: glasses, watches, pins, ID cards, slim bags, earpieces — 
used sparingly.
■ Corporate styling should remain believable enough that the slapstick violence feels more absurd by contrast.
Secretary / lobby visual role
The secretary belongs to the same visual world but should read as an intentional front-of-house silhouette: glasses, 
no tie, a slightly unbuttoned top, clean office tailoring and a more composed stance. The design should distinguish 
the role without turning into a separate art style.
Customisation principle
Customisation should swap clear graphic categories — hair mass, eyewear, tie/shirt treatment, jacket variation, 
trousers, shoes and a small number of accessories — while preserving the base silhouette system. Avoid custom parts 
that introduce excessive visual noise or break cross-building readability.
STYLISATION DETAILS
08  HANDS, FACES &amp; ANATOMY SIMPLIFICATION

Face language
■ Large, readable eyes with heavier upper lids rather than rounded “cute” eyes.
■ Expressions lean detached, tired, cocky, annoyed or self-assured rather than bubbly.
■ Mouths can be absent or extremely reduced in the base style; eyes, brows, lids and head angle carry most expression.
■ Faces should be sharper and more angular than earlier soft/cute tests.
■ Avoid dense anime facial rendering, glossy lips, detailed noses or soft baby-face proportions.
# Office snap kit

**Status:** Current · **Last updated:** 2026-11-12

The modular kit for office floors: slabs, walls, doorway, window bays, façade columns, glass partitions, the spiral 
stair, its locked door and a ceiling light.
All pieces come from one script, [kit_pieces.py](kit_pieces.py), so they share one set of grid, height and material 
values.
They are modelled to the office direction Alex approved on 2026-11-12, **White Trays + Cobalt Portal** 
([decision](concepts/Office/DECISION.md), [concepts](concepts/Office/contact_sheet.png)).
`Assets/_Project/Editor/Art/KitImport.cs` brings them into Unity; the pieces added on 2026-11-12 have not been through 
it yet.

## Build

```bash
bash ArtSource/Kit/build_all.sh <path to the blender-modeling skill>
blender -b --factory-startup -P ArtSource/Kit/assembly_test.py -- ArtSource/Kit/build <out folder>
```

`build_all.sh` builds, checks, exports and reads back every piece into `build/` and `export/<piece>/` (one FBX and a 
manifest each).
`assembly_test.py` snaps the pieces together by the convention alone, fires rays along every seam, prints PASS or FAIL 
per check and renders the result.
`build/` and `export/` are outputs; rebuild them rather than editing them.

## Snap convention

In Unity terms, 1 unit = 1 m:

| Rule | Value |
|---|---|
| Pivot | The piece's **front-left snap corner, at floor level** (the top of the slab) |
| Run | Along **+X** from the pivot |
| Thickness or depth | Toward **−Z** from the grid line. On the façade that is into the building, as in the floor 
templates |
| Inside of a room | On each piece's −Z side. A side wall running toward −Z is placed at its far end and turned −90° |
| Grid | 4 m bays, 2 m infills |
| Floor to floor | 4.5 m |
| Slab | 0.2 m thick, hanging below floor level; carpet on top, ceiling underneath |
| Floor to soffit | 4.3 m; walls and columns stop exactly there |
| Stair pieces | Pivot at the stair centre, floor level, 0° toward +Z, clockwise |
| Turns | Multiples of 90° only (cage panels: 15° + 30° steps) |

Corners close without a dedicated wall corner piece: because thickness goes inward, two walls meeting at a corner 
overlap on the inside.
The façade's end column must be `Column_CornerL` or `Column_CornerR`, not `Column_Facade`. A centred column stands 0.2 
m outside the side wall; `assembly_test.py` found this.

## Pieces

Notes for the programmer: **Structural** pieces never move and can be merged (they go under the template's Never Moves 
list). **Visual only** pieces have no collider.

| Piece | Size (m) | Role | Collider | Materials |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `Slab_4x4`, `Slab_2x4`, `Slab_4x2`, `Slab_2x2` | bay × bay × 0.2 | Structural | Box | Floor_Carpet, Ceiling_Tile, 
Wall_PaperWhite |
| `Slab_Stairwell` | 12 × 8 × 0.2, round hole r 2.65 | Structural; stair centre at local (6, 0, −4) | Use the mesh 
(not convex) | as slabs |
| `Wall_Solid_4m`, `Wall_Solid_2m` | run × 0.2 × 4.3 | Structural, opaque | Box | Wall_PaperWhite |
| `Wall_Door_4m` | 4 × 0.2 × 4.3; opening 1.2 × 2.4, centred | Structural; three parts (Left, Right, Head) | Box per 
part | Wall_PaperWhite |
| `Door_Leaf` | 1.18 × 0.04 × 2.38 | Moving; pivot on the hinge axis. In a door wall: (1.41, 0, −0.1) | Box | 
Door_Leaf |
| `Window_4m`, `Window_2m` | run × 0.2 × 4.3 | Structural sill and rails; pane is a separate object | Sill: box. 
Rails, pane: none | Wall_PaperWhite, Frame_Aluminium, Glass_Clear |
| `Column_Facade` | 0.4 × 0.24 × 4.3, centred on a grid point | Structural | Box | Wall_PaperWhite |
| `Column_CornerL`, `Column_CornerR` | 0.42 × 0.24 × 4.3, inside the corner | Structural | Box | Wall_PaperWhite |
| `Partition_Glass_4m`, `Partition_Glass_2m` | run × 0.1 × 2.5 | Fixed, see-through | Box over the whole wall (on the 
frame) | Frame_Aluminium, Glass_Clear |
