■ Avoid dense anime facial rendering, glossy lips, detailed noses or soft baby-face proportions.
Hand construction
The hand design is intentionally simplified for modelling and animation. It should read like a graphic cartoon hand 
rather than a realistic five-finger hand.
■ Three primary masses/joints: thumb, index finger, and a single joined mass representing the middle/ring/pinky group.
■ The joined three-finger mass must still imply three fingers through three subtle bumps/peaks in the silhouette.
■ Hands should not look puffy or mitten-like.
■ Avoid making the fingers long and elegant; the hand should stay compact, graphic and easy to read in action.
■ This simplification should carry consistently across male and female base models.
ENVIRONMENT
09  ENVIRONMENT ARCHITECTURE

The environment is not “cyberpunk office.” It is an idealised corporate future that never happened: clean, white, 
blue, elegant and slightly empty. Architecture should feel closer to commercial/transport design and 
turn-of-the-millennium concept renders than to gritty sci-fi.
Massing rule
LARGE FORMS FIRST
Build the room or facade from a few confident masses. Add graphic colour zones and signage second. Add small 
construction detail only where it improves scale, function or gameplay readability.

■ White or pale-grey slabs, dark window bands, cobalt panels and blue-tinted glass are the core vocabulary.
■ Curves can appear, but should be clean architectural curves rather than bubbly “aero” shapes.
■ Use bold facade graphics: a single blue rectangle across multiple floors is stronger than dozens of tiny blue props.
■ Signage can be oversized and integrated into the building skin.
■ External roads, bridges and infrastructure should feel neat and graphic rather than dirty or industrial.
■ Negative space is valuable. Do not fill every wall, corner or skyline gap.
World tone
The ideal impression is “a multinational company’s 2001 vision of 2025.” It should feel optimistic, efficient and 
expensive — then become ridiculous once players begin shooting through the windows and throwing office equipment.
INTERIORS
10  INTERIOR OFFICE LANGUAGE

The towers should combine idealised corporate futurism with recognisable office banality. The futuristic shell becomes 
more believable when it contains mundane real-world objects and slightly ugly workplace materials.
The beautiful layer
■ Glass meeting rooms and partitions.
■ White wall planes and pale modular ceiling systems.
■ Brushed aluminium, chrome details and blue-grey tinted glass.
■ Large geometric wall graphics and corporate identifiers.
■ Clean reception desks, elevator halls and long window lines.
■ Sparse cyan status lights and electronic displays.
The mundane layer
■ Grey office carpet and acoustic fabric.
■ Cheap ceiling tiles and fluorescent fixtures.
■ Printers/copiers, paper trays and filing storage.
■ Corded office phones, older monitors or early-flat-panel tech depending on department.
■ Water coolers, bins, stationery, office chairs and conference furniture.
■ Break-room and service-room equipment that looks practical rather than futuristic.
WHY THE MUNDANE MATTERS
Do not beautify every prop. The comedy is stronger when a pristine future contains the same annoying photocopier, 
carpet tiles and meeting chairs as a normal office.

SURFACE LANGUAGE
11  MATERIALS &amp; TEXTURE STRATEGY

The art direction relies more on shape, colour blocking and light than on dense texture information. Materials should 
be identifiable at a glance without requiring high-frequency surface noise.
STATUS
ITEM
HANDOVER NOTE
LOCKED
Painted wall / plastic
Smooth, pale, low-noise, broad roughness. Let light and geometry explain the surface.
LOCKED
Glass
Cool tint, controlled reflection, high transparency/readability where it affects gameplay sightlines.
LOCKED
Metal
Brushed or clean satin aluminium more often than dirty steel.
LOCKED
Carpet / fabric
Muted grey-blue, low contrast, subtle texture scale. Avoid realistic fuzz becoming visual noise.
LOCKED
Black trim
Near-black/navy anchors on frames, rails, equipment and suit-like details.
WORKING
Wear / dirt
Very restrained. Use where players touch, impact or damage objects; do not age the whole world into dystopian grime.

Texture density rule
A surface should not become interesting because of a 4K texture full of scratches. It should be interesting because of 
its proportion, silhouette, material contrast and placement in the composition. Decals are used as graphic design, 
wayfinding or believable functional markings — not filler.
GAMEPLAY READABILITY
12  WINDOWS, SIGHTLINES &amp; CROSS-TOWER COMBAT

Windows are not background dressing; they are part of the combat language. The art direction must support the “window 
wars” premise by letting teams track each other across the gap almost constantly.
■ Players must silhouette cleanly against windows and interior light.
■ Glass transparency and reflection need to be tuned for visibility first, realism second.
■ Meeting rooms can use glass walls so opponents can see activity through multiple layers.
■ Service rooms can be fully opaque. Hiding there should intentionally trade enemy visibility for the player’s own 
loss of information.
■ The main stair entrance at the centre-back of the floor should remain visually legible so opponents can understand 
when a team is progressing upward.
■ Window mullions, blinds and furniture placement must not create constant visual bars across character silhouettes.
■ Across-building architecture should frame combat rather than compete with it.
Floor readability
Open office floors should remain visually open/interconnected. Use furniture clusters and glass rooms to create local 
cover while preserving long sightlines. Dense corridor mazes work against the project’s identity and should be 
reserved for specific service/back-of-house moments rather than becoming the default.
GRAPHIC DESIGN
13  SIGNAGE, TYPOGRAPHY &amp; CORPORATE GRAPHICS

The graphic system should feel like it was designed at the same time as the buildings. UI, signs, posters, elevator 
numbers, floor maps and branding should share a turn-of-the-millennium corporate-tech grammar.
Core graphic motifs
■ Thin hairline grids and alignment rules.
■ Large cobalt rectangles or bands placed with confidence.
■ Small cyan squares, status lights and marker elements.
■ Oversized floor numbers and department identifiers.



